module gunbc.plans.dag_invocation_is_one_pattern

import std.dissolution { unbound_dissolution }
import gunbc.plan { Plan, PlanRetiresWhen, PlanIsAuthorityOnly }
import std.markdown { MarkdownBlock }
import gunbc.markdown_helpers { h2, p, li, ul, quote }

fn dag_invocation_is_one_pattern_body() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    quote(blocks: [
      p(text: "**This plan is written as an intention** (`gunbc.design_argument::plans_are_intentions`): what the repository should become, why, and done-when. The work items that realize it name this plan and are not listed here."),
      p(text: "**Grounding:** `gunbc.design_argument::single_authority`, `gunbc.design_argument::root_generalization`, `gunbc.design_argument::interface_realization_policy`, `gunbc.design_argument::declared_intent` (transparent reference, restriction, distinct identity), `gunbc.design_argument::conformance`, and `gunbc.design_ruling::operations_are_declarations_with_realizations`."),
    ]),
    h2(text: "1. Intention"),
    p(text: "**What:** when a command, workflow step or target producer requests evaluation of a declared `.dag` function, it uses one pattern: it identifies the callable in the caller's source context and binds the supplied arguments to the declaration's parameters through the existing authorities. `gunbc run` is the **caller-specified form**: the caller names entry, function and arguments under caller-supplied source roots. Every other spelling that requests such an evaluation (`gunbc build`, `gunbc converge`, each function-calling `gunbc test` producer, each CI run step) is a **command binding**: it supplies some inputs, maps its operands and options to parameters, and declares how it reports a returned value or a failure before any value exists. A spelling is an **alias** only when the operation, bound inputs, authority and observable result are all the same (a transparent reference, `gunbc.design_argument::declared_intent`). The word *nickname* keeps its existing meaning, a defect (`gunbc.product_names`)."),
    p(text: "**Why (operator, 2026-11-26):** the consolidation of seed execution routes (one demand-scoped resolve and evaluate shared by `run`, `build`, `converge` and the CliWire test producers) needs a named pattern for the next command to conform to, otherwise each new command grows its own resolve, its own exit mapping and its own vocabulary."),
    p(text: "**The anchor already exists.** `gunbc.ci.ci_run_step_target` `GunbcRunStepTarget` carries a whole `DeclarationRef`, derives the repository entry path through `gunbc.source_layout`, and its constructor is a resolution site for `v2.cli.reference_check`; `gunbc.ci.repository_ci_profile` `dag_invocation_of_run_step_target` projects it into the entry and function pair CI consumes, and that derived pair is legitimate output. The CLI argument boundary is `gunbc.cli_invoke` `NamedArgument`; arguments bind against the resolved parameter type. The defect this plan removes is a binding that **authors the path and the function independently, where they can disagree**: `gunbc.target_binding` `CliWireProgramProducer { entry, function }` and `gunbc.cli_dispatch_surface` `CliBootstrapDagOperationBinding` (an `entry_path` string beside an operation identity that already names the declaration) are today's specimens. Path derivation applies to repository-owned bindings only: direct `run` under caller-supplied roots may name an entry the repository layout deliberately gives no path."),
    p(text: "**Precedents, not homes.** `std.operation_argv` `BoundOperationInvocation` is the service-operation precedent for pairing an identity with named inputs, but its input values are text or lists of text, and its realization selects whether and how to transport the request; `v2.std.operation_realization` `OperationBinding` selects a modeled handler inside a hermetic witness frame. Neither is the home for a `.dag` function request, and a wet build needs neither a modeled handler nor its dispatch record."),
    p(text: "**What is shared and what is not.** The declaration owns its parameter and return contract. The operation or producer owns the effects and evidence it promises. The command binding owns how the caller supplies inputs and how it reports a returned value or a failure before any value exists: an evaluator error exits 1 under `run` and is `SubjectUnreached` (exit 2) under `test`; `build` and `converge` complete only by reading their receipt back; a CliWire producer refuses a function returning a bare `ProcessExit`. Merging those contracts would turn \"no reading was taken\" into \"a defect was found\" (`gunbc.design_argument::fail_closed`). Faithful execution and receipt readback once a request is admitted stay with the existing materialization / realization domain."),
    h2(text: "2. Done when"),
    p(text: "Each is assessed against the tree."),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "The pattern is a ruling in `gunbc.design_ruling` and a conformance domain in `gunbc.design_argument` `conformance_domains`, whose homes are `GunbcRunStepTarget` and `NamedArgument` (a command-binding carrier joins them once it derives its path from declaration identity), so a reviewer asks of every change that requests evaluation of a declared function whether it conforms."),
      li(text: "Every covered production call site that requests interpreted evaluation of a `.dag` function reaches the same demand-scoped resolver and evaluator, and that resolver resolves the request's combined demand once, followed by evaluation of the selected functions; no command or producer keeps its own whole-tree resolve. Interpreted reads inside a native producer count; the native process and its probe keep their own evidence contract. Shown by one before/after measurement on the same entry and arguments, and by controls: a missing bootstrap receipt still refuses, a CliWire evaluator error still reads `SubjectUnreached`, a wrong return type still refuses, and a multi-function request still resolves its combined demand once."),
      li(text: "No repository-owned binding authors an entry path and a function independently: each names its callable by declaration identity (`GunbcRunStepTarget` or its successor) and derives the path, so the two cannot disagree."),
    ]),
    h2(text: "3. Not this plan"),
    ul(items: [
      li(text: "What a bound operation does (its effects, its evidence, plan versus apply authority) stays with the operation's own module."),
      li(text: "Native-serve and native-claim producers promise evidence from a built process; they are not bindings of this pattern, though interpreted reads inside them are."),
      li(text: "Whether `std.operation_argv` `OperationRef` becomes a declaration reference is an open question: `gunbc.design_ruling::operations_are_declarations_with_realizations` settles declaration plus realization and leaves the service-identity representation open."),
      li(text: "Deploy transactions run from the lab or CI, and roadmap acceptance checked against its revision, are separate intentions."),
    ]),
  ]
}

data dag_invocation_is_one_pattern_plan: Plan = Plan {
  projection: PlanIsAuthorityOnly,
  slug: "dag-invocation-is-one-pattern",
  title: "Invoking a declared function is one pattern; commands are bindings of it",
  body: dag_invocation_is_one_pattern_body(),
  retirement: PlanRetiresWhen { condition: unbound_dissolution(description: "Retire when items 1-3 of section 2 hold on the tree and the conformance domain carries the rule.") },
}
